APX.mk = {"country":{"name":"North Macedonia","tagline":"The Vardar valley's sun-soaked Vranec, the ruins of Stobi and the Balkans' biggest winery at Kavadarci.","classification":"North Macedonia has three wine regions (Povardarie, Pčinja-Osogovo, Pelagonija-Polog) subdivided into 16 to 18 wine areas (*vinogorja*) such as Tikveš, Skopje, Veles, Ovče Pole, Gevgelija-Valandovo, Strumica-Radoviš, Kočani-Vinica, Kratovo, Kumanovo, Prilep, Bitola, Ohrid, Prespa, Tetovo and Kičevo. Labels have long used 'controlled origin' wording tied to these areas. Wine Law amendments adopted in April 2024 set up an EU-compatible system of geographical indications; as of early 2026 the first GI, 'Vardar Valley', was in preparation. Note that the EU PGI 'Macedonia' refers to Greek Macedonia."},"regions":[{"name":"Povardarie","slug":"povardarie","tagline":"The Vardar valley and Tikveš: hot, sunny, Vranec-dominated and home to nearly all the wineries.","ids":["mk-tikves"]}],"items":{"mk-tikves":{"name":"Tikveš","cat":"Vinogorje","region":"Povardarie","created":"Wine area under national law","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Vranec, Kratošija, Stanušina, Smederevka, Temjanika, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay","summary":"Tikveš is the country's most famous wine area, a sun-baked basin in the middle Vardar valley around Kavadarci, Negotino and Demir Kapija. It holds the largest share of the vineyard and the Tikveš winery (founded in 1885), whose Barovo and Bela Voda single-vineyard reds, developed with consultant Philippe Cambie, showed what the area's Vranec, Kratošija and Plavac can do. Stanušina is the local red.","producers":["Tikveš","Stobi","Bovin","Popova Kula","Chateau Kamnik"],"rslug":"povardarie"}}};
